  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAfternoon RAP analysis continues to show a stout upper level ridge \nof high pressure encompassing the central CONUS and extending into \nthe Canadian Prairie, with surface high pressure extending over the \nUpper Great Lakes from eastern Ontario and Quebec. This is resulting \nin another pleasant day across the Upper Peninsula, with mostly \nsunny skies and temperatures in the mid 70s to low 80s away from the \nlakeshores as of mid afternoon. Increased high level cloud cover \nemanating from an upstream disturbance over ND and MN tonight will \nkeep overnight temps a bit warmer than last night, with lows \ngenerally in the mid 50s to low 60s across the area. This will also \nhelp to limit fog development relative to last night, though the \nsouthcentral and eastern UP still show a 15-25% chance for some \npatchy fog through early Monday morning. High pressure remains in \ncontrol through Monday, resulting in another warm and dry, albeit \nsomewhat cloudier, day with 850 mb temps around 14-15C continuing to \nsupport high temps in the upper 70s to low 80s across the area. Any \nprecipitation should hold off over the western UP until Monday night \nwith the approach of the next system.\n\nThe upper level pattern begins to shift Monday night into Tuesday as \nthe central CONUS ridge is flattened out by a deep trough lifting \nout of the Pacific Northwest into the Canadian Prairie. This will \ninduce surface cyclogenesis in the lee of the northern Rockies on \nMonday, with a strong low then tracking to the southern shores of \nHudson Bay by Wednesday morning and driving a strong cold front \nacross the UP late Tuesday into early Wednesday. Rain and embedded \nthunderstorms will precede the front on Tuesday. Limited instability \non the order of 500 J/kg or less MUCAPE should help to preclude a \ngreater threat for strong/severe storms, but SPC does include the \nwestern third of the UP in a marginal risk for Day 3 as bulk shear \nincreases above 40 kt. Expect most of the area to receive a wetting \nrainfall from this event as latest NBM has a 65% or greater chance \nto receive at least a quarter inch for a but the far southcentral UP \n(closer to 50%) through Tuesday night. The western UP from the \nKeweenaw to the Porcupines has a 40-60% chance to receive an inch or \nmore of rain on Tuesday, but those probabilities quickly drop off to \n10-25% over the central and eastern UP. Rain should depart the \neastern UP behind the front by Wednesday morning. \n\nThis system will also make for breezy conditions from Tuesday into \nWednesday, with southerly winds increasing to 15-30 mph across a \nwide swath of the area on Tuesday ahead of the front. The strongest \ngusts on Tuesday will be in the areas favored by southerly downslope \nflow, particularly from the Huron Mountains eastward along the Lake \nSuperior shoreline. Winds will shift westerly on Wednesday, aided by \ncold air advection and pressure rises behind the front. Expect \nwidespread gusts of 20-30 mph through the day, with stronger gusts \nto 40 mph possible near Lake Superior. Will need to consider an \neventual Wind Advisory for the Keweenaw as NBM now shows a 60-80% \nchance for gusts of 45+ mph there. Meanwhile, there is also a 25-35% \nprobability for 45 mph gusts along the shoreline from east of \nMarquette to Whitefish Point.\n\nExpect calmer weather to return to the region by Thursday as high \npressure temporarily builds back over the UP behind the departing \nsystem, with highs on Thursday closer to seasonal norms in the mid \n60s to low 70s. However, the overall pattern remains unsettled as \nanother trough has the potential to bring additional rounds of \nprecipitation and breezy conditions from Friday into the weekend.",
